.TH DDATE 1 "2026-10-12" "compact-date-tools 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ddate \- decode and format a three-character compact calendar date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ddate
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I CODE
.RI [ ZONE ]
.RI [ +FORMAT ]
.br
.B ddate
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.BI \-\-date " 'CODE [ZONE]'"
.RI [ +FORMAT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ddate
decodes one case-sensitive compact Gregorian calendar day, assumes midnight
in its input zone, and displays the resulting instant in its output zone.
For example, A1B means 2010-01-11.
With no formatting option, use the installed date command's default format.
The output zone defaults to UTC.
.PP
The input zone is selected in this order: an explicit input-zone option,
a zone supplied with the code, then UTC. An explicit input flag reinterprets
the same calendar day at midnight in the requested zone.
Output-zone flags independently convert that instant for display.
.SH COMPACT FORMAT
Each position indexes
.BR 0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz .
The year index is the year minus 2000; month and day indexes are their ordinary
calendar numbers. Years 2000-2009 use 0-9, 2010-2035 use A-Z, and 2036-2061 use
a-z. Months use 1-9 and A-C; days use 1-9 and A-V.
The supported range is 2000-01-01 (011) through 2061-12-31 (zCV).
.PP
Every code must describe an existing date. A2T, meaning 2010-02-29, is invalid;
C2T, meaning 2012-02-29, is valid. Month and day zero are invalid.
The code contains no original time or zone. Decoding cannot recover those.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Print usage and exit successfully without running date or creating a log.
.TP
.BR \-V , " " \-\-version
Print the command version and exit successfully.
.TP
.BR \-d , " " \-\-debug
Create a private execution log in the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-date " INPUT"
Supply the code and optional zone as one value instead of positional input.
.TP
.BR \-u , " " \-\-utc , " " \-\-universal
Display in UTC. This is the output default.
.TP
.BR \-l , " " \-\-local
Display in the inherited system or TZ local zone.
This changes output only, leaving input interpretation unchanged.
.TP
.BR \-z " " ZONE ", " \-\-timezone " " ZONE
Display in a named IANA zone. This changes output only.
.TP
.B \-\-input\-utc
Reinterpret the decoded day as UTC midnight, overriding a supplied input zone.
.TP
.B \-\-input\-local
Reinterpret the decoded day as inherited system or TZ local midnight,
overriding a supplied input zone.
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-timezone " ZONE"
Reinterpret the decoded day as midnight in a named IANA zone,
overriding a supplied input zone.
.TP
.BR \-I [ FMT ], " " \-\-iso\-8601 [ =FMT ]
ISO 8601 output. FMT is date, hours, minutes, seconds, or ns; the default is
date. Attach optional precision, as in \-Iseconds or \-\-iso-8601=seconds.
.TP
.BR \-R , " " \-\-rfc\-email , " " \-\-rfc\-2822
RFC 5322 email output.
.TP
.BI \-\-rfc\-3339 " FMT"
RFC 3339 output. FMT is date, seconds, or ns.
.TP
.B +FORMAT
Pass a custom output expression to the installed date command.
.PP
Choose one output format: +FORMAT or one ISO/RFC option.
Choose at most one input-zone option and at most one output-zone option.
The two zone groups can be combined independently.
Unknown flags and duplicate singleton options, including aliases, are errors.
.SH INPUT ZONES
A zone supplied with the code may be an IANA name, UTC, GMT, Z, or a numeric
offset. Named zones follow the code as a separate argument or within a quoted
input. Z and numeric offsets may attach directly: A1BZ or A1B+05:30.
.PP
Offsets accept +HH, +HHMM, or +HH:MM and their negative equivalents.
Hours must be 00-23 and minutes 00-59. A separate negative offset requires
\-\- before it; attaching it to the code is simpler.
Supplied zones are validated even when an explicit input flag overrides them.
.PP
With a bare positional code, a separate positive numeric offset is the input
zone: ddate A1B +0500. With \-\-date, a separate +0500 is a literal output
format; put an input zone inside the option value: \-\-date 'A1B +0500'.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATTING
Percent directives, padding flags, widths, and locale modifiers are passed
to date unchanged. Supported directives and extensions follow the installed
implementation. Common directives include %F for YYYY-MM-DD, %T for HH:MM:SS,
%z for a numeric zone offset, %A and %B for localized names, %j for day of year,
%s for epoch seconds, %n for a newline, and %t for a tab.
.PP
Time fields describe input midnight after conversion to the output zone.
They need not show midnight or the same calendar day. Nanoseconds are zero.
Changing only the output zone leaves %s unchanged.
.PP
Midnight must exist in the input zone. A skipped local day, such as 2011-12-30
in Pacific/Apia, fails if that zone is selected for input.
Selecting that zone only for output converts an existing instant and succeeds.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ddate A1B +%F
.fi
Prints 2010-01-11.
.PP
.nf
ddate A1B Asia/Tokyo '+%F %T %z'
.fi
Prints 2010-01-10 15:00:00 +0000: Tokyo midnight displayed in UTC.
.PP
.nf
ddate A1B+05:30 '+%F %T %z'
.fi
Prints 2010-01-10 18:30:00 +0000.
.PP
.nf
ddate A1B+05:30 \-\-input-utc '+%F %T %z'
.fi
Prints 2010-01-11 00:00:00 +0000: the same day reinterpreted as UTC midnight.
.PP
.nf
TZ=America/Los_Angeles ddate A1B \-\-local '+%F %T %z'
.fi
Prints 2010-01-10 16:00:00 -0800: UTC midnight displayed locally.
.PP
.nf
TZ=America/Los_Angeles ddate A1B \-\-input-local \-\-local '+%F %T %z'
.fi
Prints 2010-01-11 00:00:00 -0800: local midnight displayed locally.
.PP
.nf
ddate A1B \-\-iso-8601=seconds
ddate A1B \-\-rfc-3339 ns
ddate A1B '+%A, %B %d, %Y'
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Locates the GNU-compatible date command. TypeScript also requires Node.js.
.TP
.B TZ
Supplies the local zone for input or output local options.
It also determines the debug-log filename timestamp.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL , " " LC_TIME , " " LANG
Inherited by date for output formatting.
.TP
.B TZDIR
Time-zone data directory when supported by the installed date implementation.
Rust also uses it to validate named zones; the default is /usr/share/zoneinfo.
.SH OUTPUT AND EXIT STATUS
Success prints the requested result and exits 0.
Errors go to stderr and exit 1. Invalid codes, invalid zones, nonexistent
midnights, conflicting flags, parser failures, timeouts, and file errors are
errors. Ordinary errors produce no decoded result. Check exit status:
a late output or log-close failure can occur after the result was written.
.SH FILES
.B _YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS_ddate.log
is created in the current directory by debug mode. Its timestamp uses local
time independently of input and output flags. Renaming the executable changes
the basename in the log filename. Files are created exclusively with mode
0600; a same-second collision fails without overwriting an existing log.
.PP
Logs record arguments, paths, zones, parsing, formatting, validation, and
errors. They may contain private input. Argument parsing happens before a log
is opened. Help and version never create one, even with debug selected.
.SH NOTES
Both implementations require a GNU-compatible date; BSD date is insufficient.
Each date call has a five-second timeout and a one-MiB limit per output pipe.
Arguments are passed without shell interpolation. No clock-setting flags or
network requests are used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cdate (1),
.BR date (1)
